|* raw_bit digitization -- noise-injected, assembled ro_array_core + sampler_dff
|*
|* Purpose: issue #21. Every other sim/ record in this repo measures ring
|* period/swing/jitter statistics (sigma_1, T_0) or deterministic (noise-off)
|* currents; none of them actually digitizes a bit. This deck drives all
|* four rings of the committed N = 4 array (design/xschem/ro_array_core.sch,
|* DR-0003's operating point) with per-stage injected transient noise, feeds
|* the combined XOR-tree output through the committed sampler_dff, and reads
|* back a run of raw bits -- the first raw-bitstream evidence this repo has.
|*
|* METHOD: rings built IN PLACE, not through the committed ro_ring5 subckt
|* -----------------------------------------------------------------------
|* design/sampler_core.spice's ro_ring5 subckt hides its internal per-stage
|* nodes inside a .subckt boundary, so a top-level trnoise() source cannot
|* be inserted in series with them without editing that GENERATED,
|* --check-guarded file (design/README.md: "Do not edit by hand"). This
|* deck instead follows sim/ro-ring-jitter-accumulation/testbench/
|* tb_ro_ring5_jitter.spice's own precedent exactly: it builds each ring
|* from the SAME primitives (ro_nand2, ro_stage), in place at the top
|* level, with an explicit trnoise() voltage source in series with every
|* stage's input (5 sources per ring, matching that testbench's own
|* topology and this repo's only prior working noise-injection pattern).
|* Everything downstream of the four ring outputs -- ro_buf, the xor2
|* combining tree, sampler_dff -- is pulled unmodified from
|* design/sampler_core.spice via /Users/rwalters/GitHub/sky130-trng/.loom/worktrees/issue-21/design/sampler_core.spice (corner-run.py's
|* --ro-ring5 override points this at sampler_core.spice for this deck;
|* see this file's own template-placeholder note at the bottom). This
|* deck never redefines ro_nand2/ro_stage/ro_buf/xor2/sampler_dff -- it
|* only skips instantiating sampler_core.spice's own ro_ring5/ro_array_core/
|* sampler_core wrapper subckts, which is why no duplicate-subckt error
|* results from combining the two.
|*
|* THE OPEN TECHNICAL QUESTION THIS DECK RESOLVES (issue #21 curation)
|* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
|* "How to inject seeded transient noise into a .tran run of the assembled
|* ring array in ngspice (trannoise vs. an explicit injected noise source)."
|* This checkout's ngspice-46 build has NO device-level transient-noise
|* option at all (confirmed by tb_ro_ring5_jitter.spice's own header during
|* issue #10 -- a purely deterministic .tran of a ring, zero injected
|* sources, converges to a bit-exact repeating period with zero seed
|* sensitivity). There is therefore no "trannoise vs explicit source"
|* choice to make on THIS build: explicit per-stage trnoise() voltage
|* sources are the only mechanism this ngspice offers, and this repo
|* already validated that exact mechanism in issue #10's own campaign.
|* This deck's only genuinely new contribution is applying that mechanism
|* to all FOUR rings of the assembled array simultaneously (rather than one
|* ring in isolation) and running the injected array through the real
|* digitizing sampler (sampler_dff) instead of stopping at a jitter
|* estimator -- i.e. closing the gap from "the noise mechanism exists and
|* is measurable" (issue #9/#10) to "here is an actual bit".
|*
|* START-UP: `.ic` + `uic`, NOT AN ENABLE/RESET-RELEASE TRANSIENT
|* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
|* This is the single most expensive lesson of this deck's own development,
|* and worth stating plainly since it contradicts the enable-release method
|* `sim/ro-array-core-combining/testbench/tb_ro_array_core.spice` uses for
|* the same assembled array: a `trnoise()` source in THIS ngspice-46 build
|* cannot coexist with ANY transient control-signal breakpoint (a `PULSE`
|* or `PWL` edge on `en`/`rst_n`, timed to release the array from a held-off
|* state) anywhere else in the same circuit, regardless of how that edge is
|* shaped. Every variant tried failed identically
|* (`ngspice: "Timestep too small... trouble with node vclk#branch"`, always
|* reported against `clk`'s own branch regardless of which OTHER signal
|* was actually transitioning, which was the first sign this was a
|* solver/breakpoint interaction rather than a modeling error in this
|* deck's own circuit): releasing enable and reset together in one 200 ps
|* edge; releasing them separated and slowed to 2-10 ns each; switching the
|* integration method to `gear`; relaxing `trtol`/`itl4`. Removing the
|* `trnoise()` sources entirely (`--noise-amp 0`, i.e. literal `trnoise(0
|* ...)` still instantiated) did NOT fail -- confirming the interaction is
|* between the mere PRESENCE of a `trnoise()`-bearing source and a later
|* breakpoint elsewhere in the circuit, not a value the noise source
|* injects. `tb_ro_array_core.spice` avoids this because it never
|* instantiates `trnoise()` at all (it is a deterministic, noise-off
|* combining-fidelity check); every OTHER `trnoise()` deck already
|* committed in this repo (`tb_ro_ring5_jitter.spice`,
|* `tb_ro_ring11_jitter.spice`, `tb_ro_ring5_swing_current.spice`) avoids it
|* from the opposite direction -- none of them has ANY transient
|* control-signal edge at all (every supply/enable is a bare `dc` source),
|* which is now understood to be why, not merely a stylistic match to
|* `.ic`/`uic`.
|*
|* This deck's fix: hold `en1..en4` and `rst_n` at `dc 1.8` for the
|* ENTIRE run (rings always enabled, sampler never reset -- no transient
|* edge on any control signal except the sample clock itself, which is
|* unavoidable) and give the four rings' own internal nodes explicit `.ic`
|* values, exactly `tb_ro_ring5_jitter.spice`'s own per-ring `.ic` ladder,
|* to kick each ring out of its symmetric DC operating point and into
|* oscillation from `t = 0` under `uic`.
|*
|* The six `sampler_dff` instances need the SAME treatment, for the same
|* reason: each is a transmission-gate master/slave latch built from
|* cross-coupled keeper structures (the `mb`/`qb` pairs), and `uic`'s
|* implicit 0 V on every one of those internal nodes is a symmetric,
|* not-necessarily-consistent starting point for a bistable feedback
|* network -- exactly the ring's own problem, one level down. This was NOT
|* obvious up front: an earlier version of this deck left the samplers at
|* their `uic` default and found (in this deck's own development) that
|* `raw_bit` read a constant ~`1.8` at every one of 24 samples despite
|* `v(xo)` -- `raw_bit`'s own `D` input -- reading near 0 V at those same
|* instants. Giving the RINGS an asymmetry-breaking `.ic` was not enough to
|* unstick a flip-flop whose OWN internal nodes had no comparable kick. The
|* fix (below, at the sampler instances) gives each one an explicit `.ic`
|* matching a valid, physically-consistent state for the topology -- the
|* exact reset-state operating point ngspice's own DC solver produced for
|* `sampler_dff` when `rst_n` was still driven low (measured directly during
|* this deck's development, before the enable/reset-release method above
|* was abandoned) -- so every instance starts as if a reset pulse had just
|* released at `t = 0`, even though `rst_n` itself never moves for the rest
|* of the run. One real consequence: this deck has no start-up RESET
|* SEQUENCE, only a reset-shaped INITIAL CONDITION -- `raw_valid`/`raw_bit`
|* start from that state and evolve under `D`/`clk` from `t = 0`, not
|* through an observable reset-release event. That is an acceptable
|* simplification for a min-entropy point-estimate campaign (this issue's
|* scope), not a claim that this is how `trng_top`/`digital/`'s own reset
|* sequencing should be modeled.
|*
|* WHY Ts = 100 ns, NOT DR-0003's Ts = 20 us -- A MEASURED, DISCLOSED
|* COMPUTE-BUDGET DEVIATION (read this before citing any H below)
|* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
|* DR-0003 specifies a 50 kHz sample clock (Ts = 20 us) precisely so ~4000
|* periods of the slowest ring elapse between samples, which is what the
|* array's sizing law assumes when it promises H0 = 0.5 bit/sample at that
|* corner. `sim/ro-array-core-combining/`'s own measured per-ring periods
|* for this exact assembled array (buffer-loaded, N = 4) run 2.0-3.6 ns
|* across the tt/ss/ff corners -- so running DR-0003's literal Ts on a
|* deck that also carries 4 rings x 5 series trnoise() sources, the
|* combining tree and the sampler was benchmarked directly ahead of
|* committing this deck at ~46 s fixed ngspice/PDK-library startup cost
|* plus ~0.12 s of wall clock per additional simulated nanosecond (measured
|* on this campaign's development machine, ngspice-46, sky130A c6d73a35,
|* TMAX = 5p, this exact 4-ring + combiner + sampler circuit -- see this
|* issue's PR description for the raw calibration runs, and note the
|* shared-machine caveat there: observed wall clock on a contended host
|* ran several times slower than this per-run figure alone predicts). One
|* raw bit at the literal Ts = 20 us therefore costs tens of minutes of
|* wall clock by itself, before any second bit; a "run of raw bits" (this
|* issue's own acceptance-criteria phrase) of even a few dozen bits per PVT
|* corner would cost hours per corner, times three corners -- genuinely
|* outside this issue's practical simulation budget on the available
|* hardware.
|*
|* This deck instead runs at Ts = 100 ns -- 200x shorter than DR-0003's
|* operating point -- solely to make a non-degenerate, multi-bit raw
|* sequence computationally tractable within that budget. At the measured
|* 2.0-3.6 ns ring periods above, ~28-50 ring periods elapse per sample at
|* this Ts -- still comfortably enough for the four rings' own frequency
|* ladder, DR-0003's wstv = 0.42/0.44/0.46/0.48 skew, to sweep the
|* combining tree through many relative phases between samples, with the
|* injected per-stage noise perturbing exactly where in that sweep any
|* given sample lands.
|*
|* CONSEQUENCE FOR WHAT THIS RECORD MAY BE CITED FOR: because the sizing
|* law's own Q_ring = sigma_1^2 * Ts / T0^3 is LINEAR in Ts, a min-entropy
|* point estimate read off THIS Ts is a measurement of a shorter-window,
|* HIGHER-rate hypothetical sampler, not of the real 50 kHz design point.
|* It is real, noise-driven, digitized evidence from the literal committed
|* array (same N = 4, same devices, same per-corner PVT), which is exactly
|* what acceptance criterion (a) asks for and what this repo's README has
|* had zero of until now -- but it is NOT a substitute for a future,
|* better-resourced campaign that runs the literal Ts = 20 us and would be
|* needed before this evidence could support any claim about the actual
|* DR-0003 operating point's H. sim/raw-bit-min-entropy/analysis/
|* raw-bit-entropy.py's own record output repeats this caveat in machine-
|* readable form so no downstream consumer can miss it.
|*
|* WHAT THIS DECK DOES NOT CLAIM: an SP 800-90B validation result of any
|* kind. See spec/porting-plan.md's own citation of gf180-trng's DR-0004
|* three-tier claim-discipline carryover -- this is Tier 2 at best (a
|* labelled, bounded, simulation-derived DESIGN ESTIMATE), never Tier 3
|* (validation, which is deferred to measured silicon per this repo's own
|* CLAUDE.md standing caveat).
|*
|* trnoise() calibration: identical to tb_ro_ring5_jitter.spice's own
|* anchor (NA = 2.0e-3 V rms, NT = 2e-10 s), i.e. the same fixed injection
|* level anchored to sim/ro-stage-noise-mechanism-check/'s measured
|* near-oscillation-band noise density. CAVEAT: same fixed-injection-level
|* caveat as every other campaign in this repo (good to ~1.5-2x, not
|* re-measured per corner).
|*
|* Single seed per corner (1), held across the whole continuous run
|* -- every bit below shares ONE noise realization per corner, exactly the
|* "one seed per PVT point" limitation sim/README.md's own campaign summary
|* already discloses for this repo's other transient-noise decks. This is
|* NOT the same as N independent Monte-Carlo draws; the standard error the
|* reduction script reports is the sample-count-limited MCV interval over
|* CORRELATED-noise-trajectory bits, not N independent trials. Read the
|* reduction record's own caveats before citing the SE.
|*
|* Digitization: v(raw_bit) is measured 1/4 of the way through each Ts
|* window, i.e. `k*Ts + 0.25*Ts` -- inside `clk`'s HIGH plateau (the
|* master's LATCHED, not transparent, phase; see the `clk` waveform note
|* above), comfortably clear of both the rising edge that just captured a
|* new value and the falling edge that starts the next transparent window
|* -- via a `meas tran bit$&k find v(raw_bit) at=<time>` loop, one
|* measurement per sample, printed as `bitK = <volts>` -- the generic
|* `NAME = VALUE` capture sim/bin/corner-run.py already performs for every
|* deck, so no harness change is needed to pull these back into the
|* record's JSON. sim/raw-bit-min-entropy/analysis/raw-bit-entropy.py
|* thresholds each at 0.5 x Vdd to recover the 0/1 sequence.
|*
|* This file is a TEMPLATE: sim/bin/corner-run.py substitutes /Users/rwalters/.volare/volare/sky130/versions/c6d73a35f524070e85faff4a6a9eef49553ebc2b/sky130A/libs.tech/combined/sky130.lib.spice,
|* tt, /Users/rwalters/GitHub/sky130-trng/.loom/worktrees/issue-21/design/sampler_core.spice (pointed at design/sampler_core.spice via
|* --ro-ring5, for its ro_nand2/ro_stage/ro_buf/xor2/sampler_dff
|* primitives), 27.0, 1.8, 1, 2.0e-3 and 40p. As
|* committed here it is not a runnable deck.
|
|.option scale=1u
|.lib "/Users/rwalters/.volare/volare/sky130/versions/c6d73a35f524070e85faff4a6a9eef49553ebc2b/sky130A/libs.tech/combined/sky130.lib.spice" tt
|
|.include "/Users/rwalters/GitHub/sky130-trng/.loom/worktrees/issue-21/design/sampler_core.spice"
|
|.temp 27.0
|.option seed=1
|
|Vdd vdd 0 dc 1.8
|Vddr1 vddr1 0 dc 1.8
|Vddr2 vddr2 0 dc 1.8
|Vddr3 vddr3 0 dc 1.8
|Vddr4 vddr4 0 dc 1.8
|
|* enables + reset: held at their ACTIVE state for the entire run -- no
|* transient edge on either, ever (see this file's own header for why any
|* control-signal breakpoint here is fatal once trnoise() sources are also
|* present in this ngspice build). Rings start oscillating from t = 0 via
|* their own .ic below, not via an enable-release event.
|Ven1 en1 0 dc 1.8
|Ven2 en2 0 dc 1.8
|Ven3 en3 0 dc 1.8
|Ven4 en4 0 dc 1.8
|Vrstn rst_n 0 dc 1.8
|
|* --- ring 1, wstv = 0.42 (DR-0003 ladder) ---
|xg1 gA1 en1 n11 vddr1 0 ro_nand2 wstv=0.42
|VnA1 gA1 ro1raw 0 dc 0 trnoise(2.0e-3 2e-10 0 0)
|x11 gB1 n21 vddr1 0 ro_stage wstv=0.42
|VnB1 gB1 n11 0 dc 0 trnoise(2.0e-3 2e-10 0 0)
|x21 gC1 n31 vddr1 0 ro_stage wstv=0.42
|VnC1 gC1 n21 0 dc 0 trnoise(2.0e-3 2e-10 0 0)
|x31 gD1 n41 vddr1 0 ro_stage wstv=0.42
|VnD1 gD1 n31 0 dc 0 trnoise(2.0e-3 2e-10 0 0)
|x41 gE1 ro1raw vddr1 0 ro_stage wstv=0.42
|VnE1 gE1 n41 0 dc 0 trnoise(2.0e-3 2e-10 0 0)
|.ic v(ro1raw)=0 v(n11)=1.8 v(n21)=0 v(n31)=1.8 v(n41)=0
|
|* --- ring 2, wstv = 0.44 ---
|xg2 gA2 en2 n12 vddr2 0 ro_nand2 wstv=0.44
